{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No statewide rent control; municipal rent control authorized","requirement":"New Jersey has no state law that establishes, governs or controls rents; municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances, which locally created boards enforce.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential rental units in municipalities that adopt a rent control or rent leveling ordinance; the publication states there is no statewide rent limit.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Municipal rent control ordinances enforced by local rent control boards"},"exemptions":"Housing owned or subsidized by HUD, HUD-insured developments with economic problems, and projects financed by the New Jersey Housing and Mortgage Finance Agency are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law 30-year exemption from local rent control","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years after completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings in municipalities with rent control ordinances; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Multiple dwellings less than 30 years from completion of construction"},"exemptions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from local rent control for 30 years; rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act notice and unconscionability limit on rent increases","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease; to increase rent the landlord must terminate the old lease with proper written notice offering a new lease at the increased rent, and the increase must not be unconscionable.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants covered by the Anti-Eviction Act; an increase must also comply with any municipal ordinance governing rent increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Rent increases in residential tenancies; municipal rent ordinances also apply"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Ban on rent increases covering conversion costs","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion; the ban applies to all tenants in the building.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, whether or not eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership"},"exemptions":"Increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are not prohibited.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"rents may not be increased to cos ts resulting solely from the conversion","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good-cause eviction requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession of a residential dwelling unit only by consent or through the legal eviction process, and only for good cause; each ground except nonpayment of rent must be described in detail in a written Notice to Quit.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence in New Jersey; in some cases a Notice to Cease must precede the Notice to Quit, and a Judgment for Possession is required before the tenant must move.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Dwelling units used as a residence"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security deposit transfer duties and withholding penalties","requirement":"A new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit plus accrued interest and is responsible for investing it and returning it. A landlord who willfully withholds a deposit from a tenant receiving state or federal financial assistance may be penalized.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 civil penalty per offense","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies; the civil penalty applies where the tenant receives state or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential security deposits; penalty tied to tenants receiving financial assistance"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 for each offense, enforced by summary proceeding; unlawfully using deposit monies is a disorderly persons offense with a fine of at least $200 or imprisonment up to 30 days, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination protection for source of lawful income","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in renting housing based on a renter's source of lawful income, along with the other protected characteristics listed in the Law Against Discrimination.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships; the law requires equal treatment in the rental of housing regardless of source of lawful income among other protected traits.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Excludes two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in owner- or resident-occupied single homes, and single-sex residences (familial status)"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner or resident-occupied single home, and residences planned exclusively for and occupied by one sex, as it pertains to familial status.","penalty":"Landlords who violate the LAD face fines and penalties up to $10,000 for a first offense; monetary damages and an order to rent the unit may also be imposed.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Landlord may charge for credit or screening report and application fees","requirement":"A landlord may charge a prospective tenant for the cost of the credit or screening report and may request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification; a denial based on the credit report requires giving the applicant the screening agency's contact information.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Prospective tenants screened by landlords for residential rental housing in New Jersey; the guide states landlords may obtain credit reports and background checks.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Prospective tenants undergoing credit and background screening"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"15 U.S.C.A. § 1681m","quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.6,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
